and real values of (n)."}, {"record_type": "example", "label": "Graph Transformation Example", "content": "What is the relationship between

\[ y=`\log`{=tex}\_3(x) \]

and

\[ y=`\log`{=tex}\_3(81x)? \]

Use the product rule:

\[ `\log`{=tex}\_3(81x) = `\log`{=tex}\_3(81)+`\log`{=tex}\_3(x). \]

Since

\[ 81=3\^4, \]

\[ `\log`{=tex}\_3(81)=4. \]

Therefore

\[ `\boxed{
\log_3(81x)=4+\log_3(x)
}`{=tex}. \]

So the graph of (y=`\log`{=tex}\_3(81x)) is the graph of
(y=`\log`{=tex}\_3(x)) shifted **up 4 units**.

The graph on page 6 marks the corresponding points

\[ (1,0) \]

and

\[ (1,4). \]"}, {"record_type": "formula", "label": "formula-1", "content": "`\boxed{\log_a(xy)=\log_a(x)+\log_a(y)}`{=tex}"}, {"record_type": "formula", "label": "formula-2", "content": "`\boxed{
